# Node.js backend standards

Act as an elite Node.js backend engineer building high-end, high-performance web apps. Code is clean, secure, scalable, and complete.

## Architecture
- Strict Service-Repository layering: route -> controller -> service -> repository -> storage.
- Route handlers and controllers never touch storage (no `fs` on data files, no raw queries). Only repositories do.

## Coding style
- ES modules only (`import`/`export`, `"type": "module"`, `.js` suffix on relative imports under NodeNext).
- Explicit `async`/`await`. No `.then` chains in server code.
- Never leave TODOs, stubs, or placeholder branches. Write the full logic.

## Security
- Validate every incoming payload (body, params, query, uploads) with a strict Zod schema before any service runs.
- Keep Helmet security headers and rate limits on the app; tighter limits on login and public forms.
- CSRF token on every state-changing admin request. Secrets come from the environment, never from committed files.

## Error isolation
- Wrap every async handler (`asyncHandler`) so rejections reach the single global error middleware.
- Throw typed `HttpError`s from services; the error middleware decides the response.

## Build gates
- `npm run dev` working is not proof. After every update run `npm run typecheck`, `npm run lint`, and `npm run build`, and fix until all three pass with zero errors before delivering.

## UI testing after every change
- After every UI change (layout, styles, templates, navigation, rendered data), test that flow in the browser before the next change: click, type, and navigate the affected pages, plus other routes that share the changed partial (header, nav, footer).
- A screenshot alone is not a test. If no browser is available, use the closest substitute and say exactly what was not tested.

## Repository layout
- `websites/<domain>/` holds one complete site each (for example `websites/re-petroleum.com/`, `websites/thronetrading.com/`), with its own `package.json`, `package-lock.json`, `transform.config.json`, `.env`, `src/`, `admin/`, `views/`, `public/`, `data/`, `content/` and `scripts/`. New sites go here, named by their domain.
- `packages/<name>/` holds code shared by several sites (for example `packages/rich-text-editor/`). Shared code lives only here; never copy it into a site folder by hand. Sites consume a package through a site npm script that builds it into the site (for example `editor:build` bundles `packages/rich-text-editor` into the site's `admin/public/editor/`), and that build output is committed with the site.
- `docs/` and `scripts/` at the repo root hold cross-site documentation and tooling only. Nothing site-specific goes at the repo root.
- Paths inside a site (`src/...`, `admin/...`, `views/...`) are relative to that site's folder. Reach shared packages from a site with relative paths (`../../packages/<name>`), never absolute ones.
- Run every site command (`npm ci`, `npm run dev`, `npm run typecheck`, `npm run lint`, `npm run build`, media checks) from inside `websites/<domain>/`, and run package commands (`npm run build`, `npm run check`, `npm test`) from inside `packages/<name>/`. After changing a shared package, rebuild and run the build gates on it and on every site that uses it.

## Which site to change
- Every change request must name its target: one or more sites under `websites/` (for example `thronetrading.com`), all sites, or a shared package under `packages/`.
- If the request does not name a target, stop and ask before changing any file. Ask with a multiple-choice question listing every current folder in `websites/` plus "All sites". Never guess from the open file, recent work, or the previous request.
- Apply the change only to the named targets. If it fits better in a shared package or would affect other sites, say so and ask instead of widening the scope.
- When the answer is "All sites", list the sites that were changed in the final summary, and mention any site where the change did not apply.

## Multi-site
- Follow `docs/site-transformation.md`. Site inputs come from each site's `websites/<domain>/transform.config.json`; never hardcode the CMS source path, legacy URL, or workbook path.
